// Test inputs for every problem in the Problems workspace. Expected answers are not stored here:
// they come from running the reference solution (SOL) on the same input, so only inputs live here.
//
//   args  Python argument lists, one string per test (Python syntax: None, True, 'strings').
//   big   a Python expression giving the argument tuple for the speed test. R is a seeded
//         random.Random; balanced_bst(n), pre_in(n) and itertools are available.
//         Sizes follow LeetCode's stated limits for each problem.
//   bigLabel  how the large input is described in the results (default: its length).
//   fast  instead of big: why there is no speed test for this problem.
//   in    how each argument is built: "list" (ListNode), "tree" (TreeNode), "lists" (a list of
//         ListNodes), "cycle" (values + the position the tail links back to), "rlist" (Node with
//         random pointers), "graph" (adjacency list, Node with neighbors), "ref" (the tree node with
//         this value, from argument 0).
//   out   how the result is shown and compared: "list", "tree", "rlist", "graph", "val" (node.val).
//   cmp   "sort" (any order), "sortall" (any order, inside each item too), "float", "inplace" /
//         "inplaceN" (compare argument N after the call), "k" (return k; first k of nums),
//         "topo" (any valid course order), "pal" (any longest palindrome),
//         "peak" (any index that is a peak), "ksort" (like "k", in any order),
//         "weighted" (random picks checked against the weights: Random Pick with Weight),
//         "reorg" (any rearrangement with no two equal letters side by side, or "" when impossible),
//         "balbst" (any height-balanced BST holding exactly these sorted values),
//         "freqsort" (letters grouped, most frequent first, ties in any order),
//         "randset" (Insert Delete GetRandom: getRandom must return a member, spread evenly).
//   api   "badversion": isBadVersion(v) is provided; args are n and the first bad version.
//         "nestediter": builds NestedInteger lists and drains NestedIterator with hasNext() / next().
//         "strcodec": Codec().decode(Codec().encode(strs)) must give strs back (encode must return a str).
//   design  LeetCode-style class tests: args are "[operations], [arguments]"; "codec" round-trips a tree.

